# Email and digests

A notification can go out by email as well as to the inbox, one at a time
or gathered into a digest. Email and push are sent by a worker running
alongside your app, which retries what fails, so a slow or broken mail
provider never holds up the request that sent the notification.

## Email

Give the notifier a [mailer](/docs/mail/overview), a way to find each
user's address, and a function that writes the email for a notification:

```gleam
let notifier =
  notifications.new(db)
  |> notifications.with_base_url("https://example.com")
  |> notifications.with_email(
    mailer,
    address: fn(user_id) { users.email_address(db, user_id) },
    render: fn(n) {
      mail.message()
      |> mail.subject(n.title)
      |> mail.text(n.body <> "\n\n" <> "https://example.com" <> option.unwrap(n.link, "/"))
    },
  )
```

`render` gives the subject and body; the recipient is filled in. An address
that cannot be found gives up on that email. Kinds whose delivery says
`EmailNow` are then emailed one by one.

### Only if not read first

Someone already looking at the app does not need an email as well.
`email_after` waits before sending, and skips the email if the
notification was read in the meantime:

```gleam
notifications.email_after(notifier, 120)
```

## Digests

Kinds whose delivery says `EmailDigest` are gathered into one email per
user, at most once per period:

```gleam
notifications.with_digest(notifier, every: 86_400, render: fn(unread) {
  mail.message()
  |> mail.subject(int.to_string(list.length(unread)) <> " new notifications")
  |> mail.template(digest_email(unread))
})
```

`render` is given the user's unread digest notifications, oldest first, up
to a hundred. Ones read in the inbox before the digest goes are left out,
and a user who has read everything gets no email. Without `with_digest`,
digest kinds are emailed one at a time.

## The worker

Add the worker to your supervision tree, before the server:

```gleam
supervisor.new(supervisor.OneForOne)
|> supervisor.add(notifications.supervised(notifier))
|> supervisor.add(howdy.supervised(app(notifier)))
|> howdy.start_application(name: "my_app_server")
```

It looks for email and push that is due every ten seconds
(`check_every` changes that), and straight away after a send from the same
node. Run it on every node: workers claim what they send in the database,
so they share the work without sending anything twice. A worker that dies
mid-send leaves its claim to expire after two minutes, and another sends
it then, so a crash can mean a second copy but never a lost one.

What fails is tried again, with the delay doubling from thirty seconds,
five times in all; then it is given up and logged. A mail provider that is
down or rate limiting is retried; one that refuses the message is not.

`deliver_due(notifier)` runs one pass and reports what it sent, for a
scheduled job instead of the worker, or for tests:

```gleam
assert notifications.deliver_due(notifier)
  == Ok(notifications.Report(emails: 1, digests: 0, pushes: 0, failed: 0))
```

## Testing

`howdy/mail/outbox` keeps mail instead of sending it, so a test can read
what went out:

```gleam
let box = outbox.start()
let notifier = notifications.new(db) |> notifications.with_email(mail.mailer(outbox.adapter(box)), ...)
notifications.draft("security.login", "New sign-in") |> notifications.send(notifier, to: user_id)
let assert Ok(_) = notifications.deliver_due(notifier)
let assert Ok(email) = outbox.latest_to(box, "ana@example.com")
```
